You will provde two versions of the prompt:

1. A comma delimited list of keywords (stable-diffusion prompt). Negative keywords (things to avoid) can be prefixed with "no " - so for example "no text".
2. Descriptive text, explaining the visual style and content of the image in long form.

For the keyword prompt, every keyword must name something a camera could photograph - an object, a material, a surface, a light source, a posture, a physical action. Keywords naming plot points, mental states, emotions as bare nouns, image format, or art style will be discarded before generation, so spending keywords on them wastes the prompt.

Write keywords as ordinary words separated by spaces - "starship bridge", not
"starship_bridge". Underscore-joined keywords are treated as single unknown words.

Do not describe the setting or any character's appearance. Both are supplied
automatically and repeating them in different words contradicts what is already there.
Describe what is happening: posture, gesture, where people are, what the light is doing.

Aim for 12 to 18 keywords. The image model's text encoder stops attending part-way through a long prompt, so keywords past that point have no effect on the image.

You must provide the keyword prompt inside <KEYWORDS></KEYWORDS> tags.
You must provide the descriptive prompt insice <DESCRIPTIVE></DESCRIPTIVE> tags.
You must provide both formats.
